The current code computes some information (`slicesByPool` and `incompletePools`) for the entire cluster upfront:

https://github.com/mortent/kubernetes/blob/2e534d6da100ef20a048ed44f9ec9997f64676a7/staging/src/k8s.io/dynamic-resource-allocation/structured/internal/stable/pools_stable.go#L60-L95

That's the new code.  It's then used here:
https://github.com/mortent/kubernetes/blob/2e534d6da100ef20a048ed44f9ec9997f64676a7/staging/src/k8s.io/dynamic-resource-allocation/structured/internal/stable/pools_stable.go#L143

What if we removed the upfront computation and instead double-checked whether a pool is really incomplete?

```Go
	// Find incomplete pools and flatten into a single slice.
	result := make([]*Pool, 0, len(pools))
	for _, pool := range pools {
		isComplete := int64(len(pool.Slices)) == pool.Slices[0].Spec.Pool.ResourceSliceCount
		// If we have all slices, we are done. If not, then we need to start looking for slices
		// which were filtered out above because their node selection made them look irrelevant
		// for the current node. This is necessary for "allocate all" mode (it rejects incomplete
		// pools) and for partitionable devices (the extra slices may contain counter set definitions
		// or information about other devices which reduce counters).
		if !isComplete {
			...
			... maybe set isComplete = true ...
		}
		pool.IsIncomplete = !isComplete
```

For the vast majority of current real-world ResourceSlices, we will directly set `isComplete := int64(len(pool.Slices)) == pool.Slices[0].Spec.Pool.ResourceSliceCount = true` because we have all slices and all of them are for the node.

> This actually becomes an even larger issue for Partitionable Devices. Since we are scoping the counter sets to a pool, the allocator needs to know about all allocated devices that consumes counters in that pool, meaning we need to know about allocated devices on other nodes than the current one.
>
> I'm wondering if an option here is to say that all ResourceSlices in a pool must have the same node selector?

We cannot make that a requirement for GA ResourceSlices because it wasn't required before. We would have to do a phased transition where we announce this, but then tolerate other slices for several releases.

I think we can instead make it a recommendation and (hopefully) with the approach above will handle ResourceSlices which follow the recommendation efficiently. The `if !isComplete` then is only necessary for the exceptions and thus rare. Or am I missing use cases where it is necessary to do the fallback regularly?

