@jhorwit2 Thanks for pinging me on this. 

@khenidak Yes, this is definitely an issue. We need to fix it asap.

The approach of using shared informers is a little confusing to me. The current informer framework only syncs with the kube-api. How do you see it applying to an external API?

I see what you've have described, and it makes sense. It's just a lot of work, and it can be error prone, I would definitely like to see a PR if you have one. Can you see the informer framework applying here?

@jhorwit2 What do you mean by moving from sync interval to queue-method? We've always been using the informer framework, which has historically done the List-Watch approach (where it queues up deltas as they come, and does a full sync periodically to ensure that state deviations do not occur due to failures). Are we using a similar approach for external calls (calls to the cloud API)?

@khenidak Could you join us at the next cloudprovider meetup on wednesday at 10AM PST? I'll be able to get a better grasp on the possible approaches to solve this. I'll invite you to the group on slack, if you aren't already on it.

In other words, tackling the comment above that line you reference
```
	// TODO: If we do just the full Resync every 5 minutes (default value)
	// that means that we may wait up to 5 minutes before even starting
	// creating a route for it. This is bad.
	// We should have a watch on node and if we observe a new node (with CIDR?)
	// trigger reconciliation for that node.
```
+1 for watching nodes; however, is adding a cache really necessary? Just have the updateFunc compare the old vs new `node.Spec.PodCIDR`. In practice, this should only change from empty to having a value. I don't think it's possible to change the pod cidr of a node, right?  The route controller doesn't care about the node's IP. 

@wlan0 I don't think anybody is proposing watching the clouds. IIUC, this is just triggering the sync based off a relevant node change or at some long interval.
